WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.760
Model output should be treated as untrusted until it is validated for the destination.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:12.180
If output becomes JSON, validate its schema, types, required fields, allowed values, and business meaning.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:18.400
If output is displayed in a user interface, use safe rendering and output encoding appropriate to the context.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:24.700
If output affects a workflow, verify that it matches policy, user authorization, and available evidence.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.680
Fluent text is not the same as verified truth or permission.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:33.060
Testing should cover prompts and workflows, not only isolated functions.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:44.320
Regression tests can check instruction hierarchy, source labeling, retrieval authorization, output format, tool argument validation, denied actions, approval gates, and safe failure behavior.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:55.980
Defensive evaluation datasets can include representative benign cases, edge cases, conflicting evidence, stale documents, and attempts to make untrusted content exceed its authority at a high level.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:02.040
The goal is repeatable confidence as prompts, models, tools, and data sources change.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.860
Logging should support investigation without creating another exposure path.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:14.880
AI application logs may include prompts, retrieved sources, tool calls, denials, validation outcomes, model responses, and user decisions.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:22.060
These records can contain secrets, personal data, customer information, source code, or sensitive business context.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:31.720
Redaction, minimization, retention limits, access control, and safe event design help operators understand behavior without storing more sensitive material than needed.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:35.060
Monitoring and incident response complete the defense.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:49.520
Watch for unusual retrieval patterns, denied actions, repeated unsafe requests, tool failures, sensitive-data access, unexpected tool arguments, high-impact approval requests, and changes in model or retrieval behavior.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:57.580
Incident response should cover prompt injection, data leakage, unsafe actions, configuration mistakes, and provider or tool failures.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:05.240
After an incident, update prompts, policies, retrieval filters, tests, logging, and training so the system improves.
